# How it works

```
Mac (Rust)                                        Android (Kotlin)
CGVirtualDisplay -> ScreenCaptureKit              USB accessory pipe or TCP
  (extra display)    (capture)  -> VideoToolbox  ====>  MediaCodec -> SurfaceView
   or main display                 H.264 (HW)
                       Quartz events  <====  pen, touch, keyboard
USB: Android Open Accessory (no USB debugging)  |  Wi-Fi: TCP  |  developer: adb reverse
```

## The virtual display

macOS has no public API for an extra display. Ligarview uses the private `CGVirtualDisplay` API, which works without SIP changes or kernel extensions. A HiDPI mode is defined in points with a pixel size of points x scale, so a 1280x800 display is 2560x1600 real pixels. Apple may change this API in a macOS update.

Mirror mode skips the virtual display: the host captures the main display instead, scaled down (never up) to fit the tablet's maximum size and with even dimensions for the encoder. The cursor shapes are scaled against the main display's size in points, and input and the supervisor treat the main display as "the display".

## Capture and encode

ScreenCaptureKit captures the display as YCbCr 420 frames and VideoToolbox encodes them in hardware. Captured frames are never queued: the encoder always takes the newest one and stale frames are dropped.

VideoToolbox defaults to 11 reference frames, which makes hardware decoders buffer 20+ frames before output. The host limits it to 1, which took decoder latency on a Tab S9 from about 500 ms to about 40 ms. On Qualcomm devices the app also sets `vendor.qti-ext-dec-picture-order.enable`, which removes the last held-back frame (about 40 ms to 8 ms at 30 fps).

The default rate control sustains about 57 fps on a busy 2560x1600 screen with about 40 ms encode latency. H.264 and HEVC measured the same latency on an M1 Pro and a Tab S9; HEVC's benefit is compression, not speed.

## The cursor

The cursor is not part of the video. The host sends its position, and its shape when it changes, on a second connection. The tablet draws it on top once per display refresh, so the cursor never waits for capture, encode and decode.

## Input from the tablet

The tablet sends pen, touch and keyboard events back on the cursor connection (TCP port + 1, or channel 3 of the USB pipe). Each is a small message:

| Message | Carries |
|---|---|
| Pointer | action (hover, down, move, up), tool, side button, position 0..1 and pressure |
| Scroll | phase, position and movement as fractions of the display |
| Pinch | phase, position and the change of the finger distance |
| Modifiers | the sidebar's held modifier keys |
| Key | one allowed key (Escape, Z, Backspace, Return, Tab, Delete, arrows) and modifiers |
| Text | typed characters |
| Edge | push the pointer to the bottom or top edge, or back to the middle |

The host decodes them and posts Quartz events. It ignores anything malformed, clamps coordinates, and does nothing unless the matching switch is on and macOS has granted Accessibility. In the other direction the host sends a small capabilities message (pen, finger, typing) so the tablet can dim what the Mac does not allow.

Two details matter on macOS. Flags posted with events (a Ctrl for zoom, a held Cmd) linger in the system's modifier state, so a Ctrl from a pinch would turn every later scroll into a zoom. The host therefore builds its events from a private event source with explicit flags, posts them at the session level, and releases the modifiers it used when a gesture, key press or link ends. And scrolling uses `CGEventCreateScrollWheelEvent2`: the original function is variadic, so on Apple Silicon its second axis cannot be passed the ordinary way.

## Wi-Fi discovery and pairing

Both sides announce themselves over Bonjour. The Mac publishes `_ligarview._tcp`; the tablet publishes `_ligarview-tablet._tcp` and listens on a random port. The tablet shows a six-digit code. When you enter it on the Mac, the Mac sends it to the tablet, and if it matches, the tablet stores it as its secret and connects to the Mac. Every later Wi-Fi connection starts with that code, and the host refuses anything else (connections from the Mac itself, as with `adb reverse`, are exempt). Unpairing clears the code on the Mac. The code is a convenience for a trusted home network, not strong authentication, and the stream is not encrypted.

## Transports

| Transport | Video | Cursor, and capabilities | Input |
|---|---|---|---|
| TCP (Wi-Fi, adb) | `port` | `port + 1` | back on the `port + 1` connection |
| USB accessory | channel 1 of one pipe | channel 2 of the same pipe | channel 3 of the same pipe |

On USB the Mac uses the Android Open Accessory protocol, so no USB debugging is needed. Frames on the pipe are `channel u8 | length u16 | payload`, at most 16000 bytes each. The streams inside are identical to the TCP ones.

## Wire format

Video starts with a 12-byte hello (magic `LGRV`, version, codec, width, height, fps), then length-prefixed Annex-B frames with flags for keyframe, repeat and heartbeat. The cursor channel carries position messages (x, y, inside), shape messages (size, hotspot, PNG) and the capabilities message. The source of truth is [`host/src/protocol.rs`](https://github.com/kykurniawan/ligarview/blob/main/host/src/protocol.rs), [`host/src/cursor_server.rs`](https://github.com/kykurniawan/ligarview/blob/main/host/src/cursor_server.rs) and, for input, [`host/src/input.rs`](https://github.com/kykurniawan/ligarview/blob/main/host/src/input.rs).

